## 5. CI Pipeline (GitHub Actions)

File: `.github/workflows/ci.yml`

```yaml
name: CI – QA & Performance

on:
  push:
    branches: [main, develop]
  pull_request:
    branches: [main, develop]

jobs:
  backend-test:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    defaults:
      run:
        working-directory: ./backend
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - name: Setup Node
        uses: actions/setup-node@v4
        with:
          node-version: '20'
          cache: 'npm'
      - run: npm ci
      - run: npm run lint   # optional eslint
      - run: npm test       # jest
      - name: Upload Jest coverage
        if: always()
        uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4
        with:
          name: jest-coverage
          path: backend/coverage

  ui-test:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    services:
      # Spin up a lightweight backend for Cypress to talk to
      backend:
        image: node:20-alpine
        env:
          NODE_ENV: test
        ports: ['3000:3000']
        options: >-
          --health-cmd "curl -f http://localhost:3000/health || exit 1"
          --health-interval 10s
          --health-timeout 5s
          --health-retries 5
        # In real repo you would mount the built backend; here we just use a placeholder
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - name: Setup Node
        uses: actions/setup-node@v4
        with:
          node-version: '20'
          cache: 'npm'
      - run: npm ci
      - working-directory: ./cypress
        run: npm ci
      - name: Start backend (dev mode)
        run: |
          cd ../backend
          npm run dev &
          BACKEND_PID=$!
          echo $! > backend.pid
          # wait for health endpoint
          for i in {1..30}; do
            if curl -s http://localhost:3000/health; then break; fi
            sleep 1
          done
      - name: Run Cypress tests
        working-directory: ./cypress
        run: npx cypress run --headless --browser chrome
      - name: Stop backend
        if: always()
        run: |
          kill $(cat ../backend/backend.pid) || true

  performance-test:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    needs: [backend-test, ui-test]   # only run if build & tests pass
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - name: Set up Docker Buildx
        uses: docker/setup-buildx-action@v2
      - name: Build backend image (for k6)
        run: |
          cd backend
          docker build -t kds-backend:test .
      - name: Run k6 load test
        run: |
          docker run --rm \
            -v $(pwd)/k6:/scripts \
            -v $(pwd)/backend:/app \
            --network host \
            loadimpact/k6 run \
            /scripts/posKdsLoad.test.js
        # k6 will exit with non‑zero if thresholds fail → job fails
```

**Key Points**

* **Backend unit tests** (`jest`) run first – they are fast and catch contract regressions.  
* **Cypress UI tests** run against a temporary backend service (same image used for k6) to validate UI latency and error handling.  
* **Performance test** runs *only* after the previous jobs succeed, ensuring we don’t waste time on a broken build.  
* The k6 job uses the same Docker image as the backend, guaranteeing the code under test is exactly what will be deployed.  
* Thresholds in `k6.options.js` enforce the ≤150 ms 95th‑percentile latency; a failure marks the job red and blocks merge.  

