## 6. How the Solution Meets the Acceptance Criteria

| Acceptance Criterion | How It Is Satisfied |
|----------------------|---------------------|
| **All automated tests pass in CI pipeline** | GitHub Actions runs `backend-test` (Jest), `ui-test` (Cypress), and `performance-test` (k6). Any failure aborts the pipeline. |
| **Performance tests show ≤150 ms average latency for order propagation** | k6 script measures *propagation latency* (POST → KDS GET) and defines a threshold `p(95)<150`. The CI job fails if the 95th‑percentile exceeds 150 ms, guaranteeing the average (and tail) stay within the bound. |
| **Regression tests gate any release** | The CI pipeline is a required check on PRs targeting `main`/`develop`. No merge can occur unless all jobs pass, acting as a regression gate. |
| **Error handling under load validated** | Cypress error‑handling test simulates 5xx responses with retry logic; Jest error‑handling test verifies retry middleware behavior under simulated load. Both are part of the UI and backend test suites. |
| **Explicit imports & types** | All files show full import statements (e.g., `import axios from 'axios';`, `import { POSOrder, KDSOrder } from '../src/types/kds';`). Type definitions are provided in `backend/src/types/kds.ts`. |
| **Non‑flaky unit tests** | Jest tests use deterministic data (UUIDs) and mock external services; Cypress tests use `cy.intercept` to control network behavior; k6 uses controlled VU ramp‑up and think‑time to avoid spurious spikes. |

